        <dl>
          <dt>Bears on</dt>
          <dd>LLM serving · metastable failures · admission control</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
      <div>
        <article class="entry">
          <h2 class="entry-title">Metastable failures in prefix-cached LLM agent serving</h2>
          <p class="entry-meta">Ali Jannesari · SwAPP Lab, Computer Science, Iowa State University · preprint, November 2026</p>
          <p>
            An agent works in a loop: model call, tool call, model call, re-sending the
            whole growing conversation each turn. Serving engines such as SGLang and
            vLLM keep that shared prefix in a GPU KV cache so a follow-up turn costs a
            fraction of the first. The cached KV belongs to sessions that sit idle in
            tool calls, and admitting waiting work evicts it. A burst of new sessions
            can therefore tip the engine into a trap in which every turn misses and the
            backlog does not drain, at a load it served before. In registered paired
            tests on Iowa State's Nova cluster, on two engines, two GPUs and two models,
            a 200-session burst left the engine at zero hits to the end in all 30 runs
            (the registered rule was met at 9 of 10 rates), every matched no-burst run
            stayed good, and a client-side session cap prevented the trap in all 30
            capped runs.
          </p>
          <p>
            The paper asks what the trapped state is. It idealizes that state as a fluid
            FIFO server on which every turn misses and proves that along FIFO orbits its
            cycles obey a linear renewal recursion. As is classical, the trap drains if
            and only if arrivals are below the all-miss capacity; above it, a Perron
            root carries every trapped state to a known self-similar regime in which the
            gap between turns grows linearly and the turns served are young and short.
            For the workload studied, sessions then complete faster than the all-miss
            capacity, so in the model a trap's measured throughput overstates the
            recovery threshold. Retrospectively, with nothing fitted to trap data, the
            theory matches 57 stuck runs; blind in outcome, runs trapped by a tool
            slowdown met 4 of 5 pre-set scores, cut traps persisted, and with a 20-step
            budget, trapped runs completed below the all-miss capacity, as predicted. Of
            two exits, refusing arrivals sheds sessions, while a session cap restores
            reuse: in registered tests it rescued 9 of 9 established traps at the same
            load without shedding. The paper does not prove that the engine converges to
            the model.
          </p>
          <p class="entry-meta">
            Preprint, with Ali Jannesari:
            <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08902"><em>Inside the Trap: A Renewal Law for Prefix-Cache Collapse in LLM Agent Serving</em></a>
            (arXiv:2611.08902). Code, data and registrations:
            <a href="https://github.com/AkshatValse/surviving-llm-stampede">surviving-llm-stampede</a>.
            Summary, abstract and citation: <a href="/research/inside-the-trap">paper page</a>.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

